\title{catIV: Instrumental Variable Analysis with a Categorical Treatment}
\description{
Identification and estimation of causal effects when an ordinal instrument
affects the choice of a categorical treatment, following the adherence set
framework and the categorical monotonicity assumption.
}
\section{Where to start}{

A typical analysis runs through four stages:
\enumerate{
\item Describe the design. \code{\link[=adherence_sets]{adherence_sets()}} enumerates the possible
adherence sets, or supply a shorter list of clinically realistic ones.
\code{\link[=response_matrix]{response_matrix()}} records which treatment each set receives under each
value of the instrument.
\item Work out what is identified. \code{\link[=projection_matrices]{projection_matrices()}} builds \code{B_t} and
\code{K_t}; \code{\link[=solve_b_pairs]{solve_b_pairs()}} finds the target populations for which a
contrast is identified, and \code{\link[=target_populations]{target_populations()}} says which adherence
sets each one contains.
\item Estimate the observable quantities from data with \code{\link[=estimate_p_z]{estimate_p_z()}} and
\code{\link[=estimate_q_z]{estimate_q_z()}}, with or without covariate adjustment.
\item Combine them. \code{\link[=estimate_p_sigma]{estimate_p_sigma()}} gives the size of each target
population and \code{\link[=estimate_late]{estimate_late()}} the local average treatment effects.
}
}

\section{Conventions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{P_Z} and \code{Q_Z} are matrices with the instrument values as row names, so
nothing depends on column position.
\item The solvers always return a matrix. A contrast with no identifiable
effect gives a matrix with zero rows.
\item Solutions are named by the adherence sets they select, for example
\code{"A4+A7"}, so results are addressed by meaning rather than by position.
\item Every instrument value must be observed in the data, so that the rows of
\code{P_Z} line up with the rows of the response matrix.
}
}

